WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.995 --> 00:00:06.580
There's no scientific evidence out there

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.550
that something like
that can really happen.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.630
It would have to be a
massive amount of fuel.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.212
Hey, everybody, I'm
meteorologist Cyrena Arnold,

00:00:14.212 --> 00:00:15.970
and I've done icing and
turbulence test flights

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.190
with NASA contractors,

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.810
installed weather stations
on the Alaska tundra,

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.490
and was the director of the world-famous

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.340
Mount Washington Observatory.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.307
Today, we're going to be
looking at storm scenes

00:00:25.307 --> 00:00:26.860
from TV and movies

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.069
and figuring out how real they are.

00:00:32.497 --> 00:00:34.070
Reporter: Oh! Oh, my God.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.530
Lisa, this tornado just came
and erased the Hollywood sign.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.010
Los Angeles isn't free of
being hit by tornadoes.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.484
They absolutely can have them.

00:00:42.484 --> 00:00:43.670
In fact, since 1950,

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.830
there have been 42 recorded
tornadoes in Los Angeles.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.650
And in 1983, South LA took a direct hit

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.058
from a rather large tornado,

00:00:51.058 --> 00:00:53.110
and several people were killed.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:54.880
The chances of getting
severe thunderstorms

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:55.850
can happen anywhere.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.580
All 50 states of the United States

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.510
have recorded tornadoes in them.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.220
We just happen to get a few more of them

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.900
in the Midwestern United States,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.000
because the ingredients for them to happen

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.219
just are there a little
bit more frequently.

00:01:07.219 --> 00:01:09.037
Newscaster: What can
you see? Is anyone hurt?

00:01:09.037 --> 00:01:10.152
Reporter: I wouldn't be surprised,

00:01:10.152 --> 00:01:11.490
there is so much damage ...

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.530
Obviously, if you're going
to be part of a media crew,

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.881
you're going to want to capture

00:01:14.881 --> 00:01:16.249
a little bit of what's going on.

00:01:16.249 --> 00:01:18.790
You see the news helicopters
flying right by the tornado,

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.510
which they do from time to time.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.950
They'll follow tornadoes
in news helicopters,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.300
but they're not going
to be very close to it,

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.267
and they're zooming in, I
think, a little bit too.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.162
Reporter: Wait, it looks like they joined

00:01:29.162 --> 00:01:30.750
and formed one large tornado.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.710
Two tornadoes merging into one.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.965
And we do see some
instances out storm chasing

00:01:34.965 --> 00:01:37.382
where you could have a
multiple-vortices tornado,

00:01:37.382 --> 00:01:39.727
and that means that you actually have

00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:42.550
smaller little tornadoes
that make up a larger one.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.010
And if the condensation in there,

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:47.013
meaning the moisture and
the waterdrops that you see,

00:01:47.013 --> 00:01:49.140
are visible only in those vortices

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.280
and not surrounding the
whole tornado itself,

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.417
it can actually make it look

00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:53.883
like there's more than one tornado

00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:55.956
in one specific location.

00:01:55.956 --> 00:01:58.006
Oh, my God!

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.010
One thing that movies
always kind of get funny

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.240
is the background clouds

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.750
in the surrounding storm
around the tornado.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.890
How dark the sky is when
there's a tornado occurring

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:12.651
actually depends a lot on
your position with the storm.

00:02:12.651 --> 00:02:14.851
If you're going to be on, say,

00:02:14.851 --> 00:02:16.347
the northeast side of the tornado

00:02:16.347 --> 00:02:17.205
and you're going to be under

00:02:17.205 --> 00:02:18.690
the main part of the thunderstorm,

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.249
it's probably going to be
really dark where you are.

00:02:21.249 --> 00:02:23.980
But there are places where
you can actually have

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.510
blue sky above you while
watching a tornado.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:27.770
Tornadoes typically occur

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.170
on the southwest side of thunderstorms,

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.878
and if you happen to be in that area

00:02:31.878 --> 00:02:34.980
and, say, southeast of that
southwest corner of the storm,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.550
you could have beautiful weather above you

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:38.340
and see the sun shining on a tornado,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.830
making the storm and the tornado

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:41.827
look white and not dark at all.

00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:44.064
Man: It's unbelievable! It's huge!

00:02:44.064 --> 00:02:45.190
I've never seen anything like ...

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.440
Yeah, I'm not exactly
sure what the meter was.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.110
I'm guessing it was more
like a seismograph-type thing

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.850
measuring the vibrations of the earth,

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.410
but that definitely wasn't an anemometer

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:54.740
measuring the wind speeds.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.574
I'd have to rate this one probably, like,

00:02:57.574 --> 00:02:58.999
a 6 or a 7.

00:03:04.211 --> 00:03:06.450
I don't think a tornado
would be strong enough

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.270
to pull creatures up out of the ocean.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.940
There is an instance in Louisiana in 1947

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.990
where it did rain down
fish on a small town,

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.740
because it was likely
picked up by a tornado.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.340
It's not going to end well for them.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.720
They're going to be pulled
up to higher elevations;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.122
they could potentially freeze,

00:03:24.122 --> 00:03:25.137
which isn't going to feel well;

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:26.410
and then they're going to be falling back

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:27.970
down to earth again
and hitting the ground.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.440
But you're talking things
that are pretty small

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:30.282
and pretty light.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.904
So, as the remnants of Hurricane Ida

00:03:38.904 --> 00:03:41.436
actually passed over
New York and New Jersey

00:03:41.436 --> 00:03:43.027
just earlier this year,

00:03:43.027 --> 00:03:45.450
there were some situations
where there was a flood warning

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.390
and a tornado warning
happening at the same time,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.060
and a warning means that it's imminent,

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:51.840
that it's actually happening right now,

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.060
not that it's going to.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.176
If I had to rate this from 1 to 10,

00:03:55.176 --> 00:03:56.760
it's a solid 1.

00:03:58.985 --> 00:04:00.902
Everybody underground, now!

00:04:02.031 --> 00:04:03.664
Don't get in a car if there's a tornado.

00:04:03.664 --> 00:04:04.770
It's a terrible idea.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.630
Don't take safety in your car, please.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:10.679
While we're storm chasing,
we're usually out in our cars.

00:04:10.679 --> 00:04:12.690
If you are in a safe place
and you're not too close

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.800
and want to get out and
take a look at it, you can,

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.450
but you want to try to stay
fairly far away from them,

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.680
you know, half mile to a mile away,

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.825
and you always want to try to be

00:04:21.825 --> 00:04:23.530
maybe on the southeast side.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.650
That's usually, for most
tornadoes, the way they move,

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.110
the safest place to be.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.857
There was a really devastating situation

00:04:29.857 --> 00:04:31.198
with a large tornado

00:04:31.198 --> 00:04:32.615
where there were four storm chasers

00:04:32.615 --> 00:04:34.230
killed in their vehicle

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.340
because the tornado changed
direction very rapidly.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.550
It grew rapidly. Its speed changed.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.108
It was very unpredictable,

00:04:41.108 --> 00:04:42.749
and it ended up killing four people

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:44.706
who were very knowledgeable
about the weather.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.590
If you're that close to it,
you need to seek shelter.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.353
You should be going to a
basement, if you have one.

00:04:52.353 --> 00:04:55.080
If you don't, try the lowest elevation

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.157
that you have in your home,

00:04:56.157 --> 00:04:58.610
and try to make it an
interior room as possible.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:01.100
So an interior bathroom
under the stairwell,

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.017
any of those places are really
good, safe places in a storm.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.030
Tornadoes at night are
something that do happen a lot,

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.550
and they're almost impossible to see.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.580
They're actually extremely dangerous.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:14.870
Sometimes the only way
you can see where they are

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:16.760
is because you're seeing
flashes of lightning

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.760
reflect off the side of the tornado

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.330
or you're getting power flashes

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:21.340
that show you where it is.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.010
Seeing the lightening right on the edges

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.720
of the tornadoes is a little more rare.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.260
We've had, there've been
some images captured

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.330
of lightning with tornadoes
happening in close proximity,

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.120
but for the most part it doesn't.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.480
Usually that's happening in
a different part of the storm

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.213
than where the tornado is located.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.377
So, a lot of people think that
it's a pressure differential

00:05:43.377 --> 00:05:45.590
that causes windows to
break and things like that

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:46.910
in a storm or when there's a tornado,

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:47.743
and it's actually not.

00:05:47.743 --> 00:05:49.500
There are some pressure
differentials there,

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.898
and sometimes people
can feel their ears pop

00:05:51.898 --> 00:05:54.440
if they're in a house and
a tornado passes over it,

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.890
but usually when you see
things break like that

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:59.524
it's because of debris
and high wind speeds.

00:05:59.524 --> 00:06:02.650
Tornadoes can pick up debris
and move it and throw it

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.560
beyond the distance of
where that tornado was.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.060
So you can have cars that
end up in trees or in homes.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.562
The static interference
that you see on the TV

00:06:10.562 --> 00:06:12.080
I think is something that
was a lot more popular

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:13.750
before everything became digital.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.810
Back then, you know, you
could see some static

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.707
or sort of some disruption in the signal

00:06:17.707 --> 00:06:19.250
as you have a big
thunderstorm approaching.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.670
May not necessarily mean
that a tornado was there,

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.680
but you could definitely get
some disruption of the signals

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.871
with a large, severe thunderstorm.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.836
So, tornadoes are pretty mysterious

00:06:33.836 --> 00:06:35.836
in that they're a very small area

00:06:35.836 --> 00:06:37.602
of very, very high wind speeds.

00:06:37.602 --> 00:06:41.120
And weaker tornadoes or
smaller ones have wind speeds

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.360
around that 100-mile-an-hour-
or-greater threshold,

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.120
but your very large EF4, EF5 tornadoes

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.970
can have wind speeds up
to 300 miles an hour,

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:50.800
and that's really, really devastating.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.830
Because you have such high
wind speeds in a small area,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.305
they can rather pick or choose

00:06:55.305 --> 00:06:56.637
what they choose to destroy.

00:06:56.637 --> 00:06:59.300
I've seen homes that are
perfectly still and untouched

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.810
with just a roof ripped off.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.845
So for it to be able
to pull off just pieces

00:07:02.845 --> 00:07:05.550
of this building that
they're in and take those

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.660
but leave other parts of
the building just fine

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:08.810
isn't uncommon.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.700
If I had to rate this clip on
accuracy, it's pretty good.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.710
I'd give it, like, a 7.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.730
That's a lot of tornadoes
very close together.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.810
I'd be running too if I saw that.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.060
The only way you can
have multiple tornadoes

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:26.185
by each other like that

00:07:26.185 --> 00:07:28.185
is sometimes when you see
a tornado on the ground

00:07:28.185 --> 00:07:29.650
it can have a satellite tornado,

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:31.390
meaning a smaller one that's actually

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.660
a part of the same storm
that is rotating around it,

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.230
but that small one is going to be tiny.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.458
It's going to look more like a

00:07:38.458 --> 00:07:40.750
waterspout type of a
tornado going around there.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:42.030
But they're really rare.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.640
I've never seen one. A
few meteorologists have.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.330
Otherwise, if you're lucky enough to see

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:48.597
multiple storms lined up

00:07:48.597 --> 00:07:50.860
producing tornadoes at the same time,

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:52.460
you might be able to see one from another,

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:54.369
but that's really a stretch.

00:07:54.369 --> 00:07:55.700
To see them this close together

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:57.590
and see that many happening at one time

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.930
is just completely not possible, at all.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:02.810
The best storm chase I've ever been on

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:06.845
was October 9 of 2001
in Cordell, Oklahoma.

00:08:06.845 --> 00:08:07.711
We were out chasing,

00:08:07.711 --> 00:08:10.194
and we saw seven different
tornadoes that day,

00:08:10.194 --> 00:08:11.090
which was phenomenal.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.223
It was a really great chase,

00:08:12.223 --> 00:08:13.331
and we're really fortunate enough

00:08:13.331 --> 00:08:14.660
to see seven different tornadoes,

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.422
including one that was an F3.

00:08:16.422 --> 00:08:18.150
Tornadoes are rated by the Fujita scale,

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.970
and now it's actually the
enhanced Fujita scale,

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:21.100
because it was updated.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.795
EF5 is the maximum that you
can reach on the EF scale.

00:08:24.795 --> 00:08:27.390
Tornadoes can absolutely
hit large metropolitan areas

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:28.933
like you see in this clip here.

00:08:28.933 --> 00:08:30.074
A lot of your tornadoes

00:08:30.074 --> 00:08:32.157
are a couple football fields wide,

00:08:32.157 --> 00:08:34.061
if that, but there are some big

00:08:34.061 --> 00:08:35.210
record-breaking ones out there.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.880
We've seen tornadoes up to a mile wide,

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:39.105
and there's even been a tornado

00:08:39.105 --> 00:08:41.500
that's 2.6 miles wide across

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:43.767
that was reported in El Reno, Oklahoma.

00:08:50.314 --> 00:08:51.907
A cold front can't be severe enough

00:08:51.907 --> 00:08:54.154
to take an airplane out
of the sky like that

00:08:54.154 --> 00:08:56.620
or freeze the ocean in
the way that you saw here.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.380
There was an instance
around Nantucket, actually,

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.150
in January of 2018

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:03.820
where temperatures got cold enough

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.110
where you actually could see some slush

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:07.660
on the top of the ocean.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.110
So if it is cold enough,

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.301
you can get some ice that forms there,

00:09:11.301 --> 00:09:13.740
but for it to freeze over
instantaneously like that,

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.082
it's just not going to happen.

00:09:20.116 --> 00:09:21.700
Cold fronts do move quickly.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.461
They are something that
we can see progress

00:09:23.461 --> 00:09:25.650
across the country at various speeds,

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.734
and sometimes they can go
50, 60 miles an hour across.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:30.961
But they just don't have that drastic

00:09:30.961 --> 00:09:32.530
temperature change with them.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.768
I have seen some cold
fronts come across Oklahoma

00:09:34.768 --> 00:09:36.451
where the backside of the cold front,

00:09:36.451 --> 00:09:38.217
it was in the teens,
and on the front side,

00:09:38.217 --> 00:09:40.460
it was just close to 70 degrees.

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:43.896
That's a really substantial
and record-breaking cold front.

00:09:43.896 --> 00:09:45.403
But to see things, you know,

00:09:45.403 --> 00:09:47.128
happen like you saw here in this clip,

00:09:47.128 --> 00:09:48.628
where people are freezing

00:09:48.628 --> 00:09:50.330
or water's freezing instantly,

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.290
it's just not possible for that to happen.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:55.020
To be that extreme just isn't possible.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.126
It'd have to get, like, a 2 from me.

00:09:57.126 --> 00:09:59.080
Cold fronts are real, and they do exist.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.330
They're not this extreme.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:03.005
And there was a tornado,

00:10:03.005 --> 00:10:05.923
but it was just a few too many of them.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.240
The way this tornado does
the damage to this farm

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.273
and to that building is
actually pretty accurate.

00:10:20.478 --> 00:10:22.150
Having a tornado that far away

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:25.650
actually pull someone up
into it isn't realistic.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:28.540
Very few people have ever been able to say

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:30.400
they've seen the inside of a tornado.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:31.895
I don't know of anybody personally

00:10:31.895 --> 00:10:35.150
who's been pulled up into
a tornado inside their car

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.840
and has lived to tell about it.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.182
There have been a lot of people

00:10:38.182 --> 00:10:40.843
who have taken some glancing
blows from tornadoes

00:10:40.843 --> 00:10:43.173
or have been spun around
on the ground in their car

00:10:43.173 --> 00:10:44.723
while the tornado passes over,

00:10:44.723 --> 00:10:48.344
but no instances of someone
being pulled up in their car

00:10:48.344 --> 00:10:51.727
into the tornado and then
coming out the top of the storm.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.310
There's no scientific evidence out there

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:00.290
that something like
that can really happen.

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:03.329
It would have to be a
massive amount of fuel.

00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:05.700
You do see tornadoes that
are made up of fire, though.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.050
Actually, a lot of times
when there are forest fires,

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:09.850
you get things called fire whirls,

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:11.423
which are like a dust devil,

00:11:11.423 --> 00:11:13.465
and they can encompass flames

00:11:13.465 --> 00:11:15.490
and actually be caused by the fire itself.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.490
So that's the closest thing I can think of

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:18.605
to what you saw here.

00:11:18.605 --> 00:11:20.298
They looked a little bit more realistic

00:11:20.298 --> 00:11:22.460
than when you see in some other movies,

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:24.790
but coming out up the top of the tornado

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.930
and stuff like that, it's
a little bit of a stretch.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:28.605
I'd give it, like, a 5.

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:38.330
Was he hanging on by his tongue?

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:39.490
Producer: Yeah, I believe so.

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:40.323
OK.

00:11:40.323 --> 00:11:43.935
You can take your weight in
pounds, cut that in half,

00:11:43.935 --> 00:11:46.306
and that'll be the speed in miles per hour

00:11:46.306 --> 00:11:48.570
that you'll be able to walk around, max,

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:49.650
without getting blown over.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.909
So if you weigh 150 pounds,

00:11:51.909 --> 00:11:53.970
it's going to be about
75-mile-an-hour winds

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:54.983
are going to be the max

00:11:54.983 --> 00:11:56.610
that you're going to stay standing for.

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:58.950
They're showing people being
pulled up and sucked up.

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:00.380
So, typically if you think about, say,

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.210
a skydiver falling out of an airplane

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:03.470
and they're free-falling,

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.210
they reach what's called
terminal velocity.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:08.539
That's around 120 to 125 miles an hour.

00:12:08.539 --> 00:12:10.665
So in order to pick somebody up again

00:12:10.665 --> 00:12:12.506
and do that and be taking them,

00:12:12.506 --> 00:12:13.860
the wind speeds would have to be

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.710
at least 120 to 125 miles an hour,

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:18.810
and how quickly they'd be moving past that

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.248
would have to be added on to that amount.

00:12:21.248 --> 00:12:23.730
So if you want to see
someone fly away from you

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.730
at 25 miles an hour, you're going to need

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:28.297
about 150-mile-an-hour winds to do so.

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:33.968
Lightning is really dangerous.

00:12:33.968 --> 00:12:37.930
It can actually occur 30
miles outside of a storm.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:40.110
So where you have your storm happening,

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.520
lightning can go beyond it.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.470
So that's why there's
a lot of these rules,

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:45.120
like the 30-30 safety rule.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:48.079
So after you see the
last flash of lightning,

00:12:48.079 --> 00:12:50.780
wait 30 seconds until
you hear that thunder.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:53.454
So then you know that that's
at least six miles away.

00:12:53.454 --> 00:12:56.400
Then wait another 30 minutes
before you go outside.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.080
And this is really
critical for weather safety

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.610
to make sure you don't
get hit by lightning,

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.320
'cause thousands of people are
hit by lightning every year.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:03.730
I mean, it's got to be a 1.

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:05.640
I don't know anyone who's
got the power of weather,

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.666
and I also don't know
anyone who can hold on

00:13:07.666 --> 00:13:10.041
in 120-mile-an-hour
winds with their tongue.

00:13:11.337 --> 00:13:12.220
To your right!

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:13.728
I'm going to get the transistor radio

00:13:13.728 --> 00:13:16.251
to see if I can get the weather channel!

00:13:16.251 --> 00:13:17.908
What does that thing mean?

00:13:19.541 --> 00:13:21.041
There were a lot of cues in that

00:13:21.041 --> 00:13:22.420
to go somewhere safe

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.600
and not just be messing
around on the staircase.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.933
There were sirens going off,

00:13:26.933 --> 00:13:28.310
there was the loss of power,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.600
there was the tornado
warning on the television.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.480
All things that people
need to pay attention to,

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:34.460
and those are all serious.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:35.718
You need to go take shelter

00:13:35.718 --> 00:13:37.151
when those things are happening.

00:13:37.151 --> 00:13:39.364
[sirens]

00:13:39.364 --> 00:13:41.322
So, the sirens you hear in the background

00:13:41.322 --> 00:13:44.210
are common in areas
that are tornado-prone.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.470
So if there's areas that
are likely to get tornadoes

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.990
in the Midwest, a lot of
towns have tornado sirens.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:51.010
Now, the average for the
National Weather Service

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.340
is somewhere between eight
and 11 minutes of warning

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:54.756
before the tornado occurs,

00:13:54.756 --> 00:13:56.423
letting you know that it's on its way.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.500
The way they showed the
inside of the house moving,

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.314
you know, you could see some vibrations

00:14:03.314 --> 00:14:06.253
and things like that
happening during a tornado.

00:14:06.253 --> 00:14:07.144
If it's passing by,

00:14:07.144 --> 00:14:09.310
it could last for a fairly
long period of time,

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:10.301
like they showed here.

00:14:10.301 --> 00:14:11.210
I'd give it about a 7.

